  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss over x, z in [-2, 2]. Steeper in x, so overshoot starts there once learning rate > 0.4 (0.8 for divergence). Start (1.3, 1.6).",
    "Noise: Gaussian, centred on the true slope, sd = 3·sqrt((64−B)/(B·63)) for batch size B, so it is exactly 0 at 64. It continues through the seeded sequence across runs, and Reset replays it from the start.",
    "Arrows are drawn at true step length (scale 1, bowl units). Orange: next mini-batch step = −rate × (slope + noise), the way the ball moves. Grey: −rate × true slope. Both hide when a run ends.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when loss > 8 (diverged). The ball and trails are capped at the bowl edge; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "While playing, one update happens every 5 host steps (6 per second at speed 1). Take one step does exactly one update. Changing a setting affects the next steps."
  ]
}